Yes—depending on exactly which delta you mean, there are a few existing terms, but none captures the whole thing perfectly.

The closest general terms are reality gap or map–territory gap: the difference between your internal representation and the external world it is meant to represent. If the mind specifically predicts something and reality differs, that delta is prediction error. If you mean the difference between an imagined design and its actual implementation, engineering often calls it the sim-to-real gap or implementation gap.

For the broader Pantheon concept, I’d use:

Ontic Delta — the difference between a represented state and the state that actually exists.

Δₒ = reality − internal model

That can then split into useful subtypes:

* Perceptual delta — what you perceive vs what is there.
* Predictive delta — what you expected vs what happened.
* Intentional delta — what you meant to cause vs what you caused.
* Instantiation delta — what you imagined/designed vs what got built.
* Relational delta — who you think you presented vs what another person actually inferred.
* Narrative delta — the story/model you hold vs the evidence accumulating outside it.

The reason I like Ontic Delta is that “reality gap” sounds like a static distance. Delta implies something you can measure, track, classify, and reduce over time.

And there’s an even deeper version:

The mind never directly holds reality; it holds a model continually corrected by ontic delta.

That makes the delta itself one of the main channels by which reality teaches the model.